Set Up Active Directory
Step 1: Set a Static IP Address
Before installing Active Directory, assign a static IP so the server's address does not change.

Before picking an IP, check your current network range. Open Command Prompt and run:

ipconfig
If your current IP is something like 192.168.1.x, then 192.168.1.50 is fine. If it is 10.0.0.x, use something like 10.0.0.50 instead.

Open Server Manager

Click Local Server on the left

Next to Ethernet, click the blue network link

Right-click your network adapter (usually Ethernet0)

Click Properties

Select Internet Protocol Version 4 (TCP/IPv4)

Click Properties

Select Use the following IP address

Enter your static IP settings — example: | Field | Example Value | |---|---| | IP Address | 192.168.1.50 | | Subnet Mask | 255.255.255.0 | | Default Gateway | 192.168.1.1 | | Preferred DNS | 192.168.1.50 | | Alternate DNS | 8.8.8.8 |

Click OK to save

Set the Preferred DNS to the server's own IP address. Domain Controllers run their own DNS service, so the server needs to point to itself.

Screenshots

Step 2: Add Roles and Features
Open Server Manager
Click Manage in the top-right corner
Select Add Roles and Features
Click Next on the Before You Begin page
Select Role-based or feature-based installation > click Next
Choose your local server (e.g., DC01) > click Next
Screenshots

Step 3: Enable Active Directory Domain Services
On the server roles page, check Active Directory Domain Services
When prompted, click Add Features
Click Next through the Features page (leave defaults)
Click Next through the AD DS info page
Click Install on the confirmation page
Wait for the installation to complete
The server is not yet a Domain Controller — that happens in the next step.

Screenshots

Step 4: Promote to Domain Controller
In Server Manager, click the notification flag in the top-right corner
Select Promote this server to a domain controller
On the deployment page, select Add a new forest
Enter the root domain name: corp.local > click Next
Screenshots

Step 5: Configure Domain Controller Options
Leave DNS server and Global Catalog (GC) checked
Set a Directory Services Restore Mode (DSRM) password
Save this password somewhere safe — it is used for recovery and is separate from the admin password
Click Next
Screenshots

Step 6: Finish Configuration
DNS Options — you may see a delegation warning, this is normal in a lab > click Next
NetBIOS name — confirm it shows CORP > click Next
Paths — leave the default database, log, and SYSVOL paths > click Next
Review — confirm your settings > click Next
Wait for prerequisite checks to pass > click Install The server will now configure Active Directory, install DNS, create the forest, and promote itself to a Domain Controller.
Screenshots

Step 7: Restart and Log In
The server will restart automatically after installation
At the login screen, log in as the Domain Administrator:
CORP\Administrator
or

Administrator@corp.local

Domain Join Experience
Simulate joining a Windows client machine to your domain.

Steps:

Set up a second VM running Windows 10 or 11
Set its DNS server to the IP address of your Domain Controller
Go to Settings > System > About > Domain or workgroup
Click Change and enter your domain name (e.g., corp.local)
Enter Domain Administrator credentials when prompted
Restart the VM
Log in using a domain user account (e.g., corp\jsmith)

Group Policy Management
Group Policies let you enforce settings and rules across users and computers in the domain.

Steps:

Open Group Policy Management from Server Manager > Tools
Expand your domain > right-click Group Policy Objects > New
Name the policy (e.g., Password Policy, Desktop Lockdown)
Right-click the policy > Edit to open the Group Policy Editor
Navigate to the setting you want to configure (e.g., Computer Configuration > Policies > Windows Settings > Security Settings)
Link the policy to an OU by dragging it or right-clicking the OU > Link an Existing GPO
Run gpupdate /force on a client machine to apply changes immediately

Organizational Units (OUs)
OUs let you organize users, computers, and groups into logical containers — similar to folders.

Steps:

In ADUC, right-click your domain > New > Organizational Unit
Name the OU (e.g., IT Department, HR, Finance)
Drag and drop users or computers into the appropriate OU
OUs can have Group Policies applied to them individually

User & Group Management
Create and manage user accounts and groups inside Active Directory Users and Computers (ADUC).

Steps:

Open Active Directory Users and Computers from Server Manager > Tools
Expand your domain (e.g., corp.local)
Right-click Users > New > User
Fill in the first name, last name, and username (e.g., jsmith)
Set a password and configure password settings
Click Finish to create the user
To create a group, right-click Users > New > Group
Name the group (e.g., IT Support), set scope to Global and type to Security
Open the group > Members tab > Add to assign users
